## Running it locally

Open `defend-your-experience.html` in any browser. No build step, no server, no API key, no backend — one file.

Outside the Claude artifact runtime the app detects that `window.claude` is absent and drops into **Local Drill Mode**: claim extraction runs on heuristics (numeric density, hedge-word detection, first-person ownership, mechanism markers) and questions come from a built-in probe bank keyed to claim category. Every feature still works — the scoring is rules-based instead of model-based. The status chip in the header tells you which mode you are in.

## Architecture

| Layer | Approach |
| --- | --- |
| Runtime | Vanilla HTML + CSS + JS, single file, zero dependencies |
| Model access | `claude.use("sample")` → `sample.json()` for structured verdicts, `sample()` for the prose debrief |
| Claim extraction | One `sample.json` call returning `{claim, category, attack, evidence_needed}[]`, cached 15 min |
| Interrogation loop | Per-claim turn array replayed as `sample.json` messages, `cache: false`, `AbortController` per call |
| Scoring | Weighted blend — new verdict 65%, running score 35% — so one strong answer cannot erase a weak history |
| Degradation | Every model call has a heuristic fallback; `not_granted` / `sampling_disabled` silently switch modes mid-session |
| Persistence | `localStorage`, wrapped in try/catch so blocked storage never breaks the page |
| Export | `claude.use("downloads")` when available, clipboard + visible Markdown block otherwise |

### Design

Dark cinematic console. Chakra Petch for display, IBM Plex Sans for body, IBM Plex Mono for data readouts. Teal-cyan accent against a blue-biased charcoal, with a semantic three-colour state system (`held` / `shaky` / `exposed`) kept separate from the accent hue so status reads at a glance without competing with the interface. Claim ledger on the left as a scannable stripe list, transcript on the right — the layout of an operations console, not a chat app.

## Key learnings

**1. The framing is the whole product.** "Review my résumé" and "defend your résumé" produce completely different applications from nearly the same input. A reviewer optimises the document; an adversary optimises *you*. Once the prompt establishes that every sentence is a claim under test, the model stops being agreeable — and that is exactly what makes the practice useful.

**2. Structured output is what makes a conversation adaptive.** Returning free prose gives you a chatbot. Returning `{score, note, gap, next, fix}` gives you a state machine: the score drives the meter, the gap accumulates into the report, and `next` becomes the following question. The JSON contract is where "adaptive" actually lives.

**3. Scoring must be stingy or the whole exercise collapses.** The first version handed out 70s for anything articulate. Adding "80+ only for an answer with specifics a liar could not invent" to the judge instructions changed the distribution completely — and made the Defense Integrity number mean something.

**4. Build the offline path first, not last.** Designing the heuristic drill mode up front forced clear thinking about what each model call was actually *for*. It also means the deliverable is a genuinely standalone HTML file rather than a shell that only works inside one runtime.

**5. Weighted scoring beats last-answer-wins.** Blending each new verdict with the running score at 65/35 stops a single rehearsed answer from redeeming a claim that fell apart twice. Interview confidence is cumulative; the metric should be too.

**6. Untested is not neutral.** Counting untested claims as zero in the integrity roll-up was a deliberate choice. A résumé line nobody has pressured you on is not a strength — it is an unknown, and unknowns are what sink interviews.

